0001- /*
0002- FUNCTION
0003- <<rand>>, <<srand>>---pseudo-random numbers
0004- INDEX
0005- 	rand
0006- INDEX
0007- 	srand
0008- INDEX
0009- 	rand_r
0010- ANSI_SYNOPSIS
0011- 	#include <stdlib.h>
0012- 	int rand(void);
0013- 	void srand(unsigned int <[seed]>);
0014- 	int rand_r(unsigned int *<[seed]>);
0015- TRAD_SYNOPSIS
0016- 	#include <stdlib.h>
0017- 	int rand();
0018- 	void srand(<[seed]>)
0019- 	unsigned int <[seed]>;
0020- 	void rand_r(<[seed]>)
0021- 	unsigned int *<[seed]>;
0022- DESCRIPTION
0023- <<rand>> returns a different integer each time it is called; each
0024- integer is chosen by an algorithm designed to be unpredictable, so
0025- that you can use <<rand>> when you require a random number.
0026- The algorithm depends on a static variable called the ``random seed'';
0027- starting with a given value of the random seed always produces the
0028- same sequence of numbers in successive calls to <<rand>>.
0029- You can set the random seed using <<srand>>; it does nothing beyond
0030- storing its argument in the static variable used by <<rand>>.  You can
0031- exploit this to make the pseudo-random sequence less predictable, if
0032- you wish, by using some other unpredictable value (often the least
0033- significant parts of a time-varying value) as the random seed before
0034- beginning a sequence of calls to <<rand>>; or, if you wish to ensure
0035- (for example, while debugging) that successive runs of your program
0036- use the same ``random'' numbers, you can use <<srand>> to set the same
0037- random seed at the outset.
0038- RETURNS
0039- <<rand>> returns the next pseudo-random integer in sequence; it is a
0040- number between <<0>> and <<RAND_MAX>> (inclusive).
0041- <<srand>> does not return a result.
0042- NOTES
0043- <<rand>> and <<srand>> are unsafe for multi-thread applications.
0044- <<rand_r>> is MT-Safe and should be used instead.
0045- PORTABILITY
0046- <<rand>> is required by ANSI, but the algorithm for pseudo-random
0047- number generation is not specified; therefore, even if you use
0048- the same random seed, you cannot expect the same sequence of results
0049- on two different systems.
0050- <<rand>> requires no supporting OS subroutines.
0051- */
0052- #include <stdlib.h>
0053- static unsigned int next = 1;
0054- void
0055- _DEFUN (srand, (seed), unsigned int seed)
0056- {
0057-         next = seed;
